{"date": "2026-10-10", "post_id": 1430702, "title": "Lunar Farside", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-10-lunar-farside/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Tidally locked in <a href=\"http://geosteph-adventuresinearthandspace.blogspot.com/2008/02/other-side-of-moon-synchronous-rotation.html\">synchronous rotation</a>, the <a href=\"https://lroc.im-ldi.com/educators/history\">Moon always presents</a> its familiar <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=110303\">nearside</a> to <a href=\"https://www.artchive.com/artwork/a-sunday-afternoon-on-the-island-of-la-grande-jatte-georges-seurat-1884-1886/\">denizens of planet Earth</a>. From lunar orbit, the Moon's farside can become familiar, though. In fact <a href=\"https://lroc.im-ldi.com/images/298\">this sharp picture</a>, a mosaic from the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter's wide angle camera, is centered on the lunar farside. Part of a <a href=\"https://data.lroc.im-ldi.com/lroc/view_rdr/WAC_GLOBAL\">global mosaic</a> of over 15,000 images acquired between November 2009 and February 2011, the highest resolution version shows features at a scale of 100 meters per pixel. Surprisingly, the rough and battered surface of the farside looks very different from the nearside covered with smooth dark lunar maria. A likely explanation is that the <a href=\"https://www.planetary.org/articles/the-two-faced-moon\">farside crust is thicker</a>, making it harder for molten material from the interior to flow to the surface and form dark, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=260725\">smooth maria</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>Sunday's Childe", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> / <a href=\"https://www.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">GSFC</a> / Arizona State Univ. / <a href=\"https://lroc.sese.asu.edu/index.html\">Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> / <a href=\"https://www.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">GSFC</a> / Arizona State Univ. / <a href=\"https://lroc.sese.asu.edu/index.html\">Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter</a>", "alt": "A mosaic image of the farside of the Moon.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-10-lunar-farside/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/farside_lro1600.jpg?w=1600&h=1600&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 10 - Lunar Farside</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-10-lunar-farside/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Tidally locked in synchronous rotation, the Moon always presents its familiar nearside to denizens of planet Earth. From lunar orbit, the Moon&#039;s farside can become familiar, though. In fact this sharp picture, a mosaic from the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter&#039;s wide angle camera, is centered on the lunar farside. Part of a global mosaic of over 15,000 images acquired between November 2009 and February 2011, the highest resolution version shows features at a scale of 100 meters per pixel. Surprisingly, the rough and battered surface of the farside looks very different from the nearside covered with smooth dark lunar maria. A likely explanation is that the farside crust is thicker, making it harder for molten material from the interior to flow to the surface and form dark, smooth maria.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: Sunday&#039;s Childe\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"lunar farside\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Lunar Farside\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-10-lunar-farside/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/farside_lro1600.jpg?w=1600&#038;h=1600&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/farside_lro1600.jpg?w=1600&#038;h=1600&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A mosaic image of the farside of the Moon.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-10T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-07T09:22:54-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Lunar Farside\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/farside_lro1600.jpg?w=1600&#038;h=1600&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 10<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/farside_lro1600.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/farside_lro1600.jpg\" alt=\"A mosaic image of the farside of the Moon.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Lunar Farside </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> / <a href=\"https://www.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">GSFC</a> / Arizona State Univ. / <a href=\"https://lroc.sese.asu.edu/index.html\">Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Tidally locked in <a href=\"http://geosteph-adventuresinearthandspace.blogspot.com/2008/02/other-side-of-moon-synchronous-rotation.html\">synchronous rotation</a>, the <a href=\"https://lroc.im-ldi.com/educators/history\">Moon always presents</a> its familiar <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=110303\">nearside</a> to <a href=\"https://www.artchive.com/artwork/a-sunday-afternoon-on-the-island-of-la-grande-jatte-georges-seurat-1884-1886/\">denizens of planet Earth</a>. From lunar orbit, the Moon's farside can become familiar, though. In fact <a href=\"https://lroc.im-ldi.com/images/298\">this sharp picture</a>, a mosaic from the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter's wide angle camera, is centered on the lunar farside. Part of a <a href=\"https://data.lroc.im-ldi.com/lroc/view_rdr/WAC_GLOBAL\">global mosaic</a> of over 15,000 images acquired between November 2009 and February 2011, the highest resolution version shows features at a scale of 100 meters per pixel. Surprisingly, the rough and battered surface of the farside looks very different from the nearside covered with smooth dark lunar maria. A likely explanation is that the <a href=\"https://www.planetary.org/articles/the-two-faced-moon\">farside crust is thicker</a>, making it harder for molten material from the interior to flow to the surface and form dark, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=260725\">smooth maria</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>Sunday's Childe\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-9-stickney-crater/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nJerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261010/html"}